{"id": "fresh-0", "phase": "fresh", "decode_tok_s": 3.089784802663184, "ttft_seconds": 10.74467504399945, "text": "# Database Transaction Recovery After Power Failure\n\nA power failure is the canonical \"crash\" scenario that database systems are designed to survive. When the machine loses power, everything in volatile memory \u2014 the buffer pool, the log tail, in-flight transaction state \u2014 vanishes instantly. Yet a well-designed database must guarantee that committed transactions survive and uncommitted ones leave no trace. This article explains the mechanisms that make that possible, walks through concrete examples, and discusses where the guarantees break down.\n\n## The Two Core Guarantees: Atomicity and Durability\n\nRecovery exists to uphold two of the ACID properties:\n\n- **Atomicity"}
{"id": "fresh-1", "phase": "fresh", "decode_tok_s": 3.7345118461555007, "ttft_seconds": 10.836804091006343, "text": "# How a Compiler Detects and Optimizes Repeated Expressions\n\nRepeated expressions\u2014computing the same value more than once\u2014are among the most common sources of wasted work in programs. Compilers attack this problem with a family of techniques collectively called **redundancy elimination**, of which **common subexpression elimination (CSE)** is the canonical member. This article explains how a compiler *detects* repeated expressions, how it *optimizes* them, walks through concrete worked examples at several levels (local, global, and partial redundancy), and discusses the limitations that keep these optimizations from being universally applicable.\n\n## 1"}
{"id": "fresh-2", "phase": "fresh", "decode_tok_s": 3.6798007709689724, "ttft_seconds": 11.194251560002158, "text": "# TCP Congestion Control: Adaptation to Changing Bandwidth\n\nTCP congestion control is the set of algorithms that governs how aggressively a sender injects packets into the network. Its central challenge is that the network's available bandwidth is unknown, time-varying, and shared with other flows. TCP must therefore *probe* for capacity, back off when it overshoots, and continuously re-probe as conditions change. This essay explains the mechanisms by which TCP adapts, works through concrete numerical examples, and discusses the limitations of the approach.\n\n## The Core Mechanisms\n\nModern TCP (Reno/NewReno, and its descendants CUBIC"}
